(function(m,c){var L=window.ACADEMY.lessons;for(var id in c){Object.assign(L[id],c[id]);}(window.ACADEMY_LOADED=window.ACADEMY_LOADED||{})[m]=1;})("cloud-iam",{"cloud-iam/identities-principals-and-permissions":{"html":"\u003cp>Every cloud action is a request: someone or something asks to do an action on a resource. The cloud checks identity, then permissions, then conditions, and answers allow or deny. Almost every security problem in the cloud is a mistake in that chain: the wrong identity, too many permissions, or a condition nobody checked. This lesson gives you the vocabulary before the provider-specific lessons.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Separate authentication from authorization\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Name the parts of an access decision\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Tell human identities from workload identities\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Spot the most common identity mistakes\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"authentication-and-authorization\">Authentication and authorization\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Authentication\u003c/strong> answers &quot;who are you?&quot; with a password, a hardware key, a token or a certificate. \u003cstrong>Authorization\u003c/strong> answers &quot;what may you do?&quot; by comparing the request with policies. A system can authenticate you correctly and still wrongly authorize you, and the second mistake is the common one. Multi-factor authentication protects the first step; least privilege protects the second.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-parts-of-a-decision\">The parts of a decision\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Think of four fields. The \u003cstrong>principal\u003c/strong> is who asks (a person, a service, a pipeline). The \u003cstrong>action\u003c/strong> is what they want (read an object, start a server). The \u003cstrong>resource\u003c/strong> is the thing acted on (one bucket, one subscription). The \u003cstrong>conditions\u003c/strong> narrow it further (only from this network, only today, only with MFA). A policy is a statement that allows or denies a combination of these. Most clouds start from deny by default: with no matching allow, the answer is no. An explicit deny beats any allow.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"humans-and-workloads\">Humans and workloads\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>People sign in through a central identity provider, ideally with single sign-on and MFA, and receive short-lived sessions. Workloads such as a virtual machine, a function or a Kubernetes pod need identities too. The safe pattern is an identity that the platform attaches to the workload, which gives it temporary credentials automatically. The unsafe pattern is a long-lived access key copied into a configuration file or a repository, where it is leaked, shared and never rotated.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-the-parts-fit-together\">How the parts fit together\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"n1&gt;n2;n2&gt;n3\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">How Identities, principals and permissions fits together\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"16.7\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"83.3\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n1\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:16.7%;top:25.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Authentication and authorization\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n2\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:75.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">The parts of a decision\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-edge\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n3\" data-kind=\"edge\" style=\"left:83.3%;top:25.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Humans and workloads\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Network edge\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Authentication and authorization to The parts of a decision; 2, The parts of a decision to Humans and workloads.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-security\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Security\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-edge\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Network edge\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n1\">\u003cdt>Authentication and authorization \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Authentication answers &quot;who are you?&quot; with a password, a hardware key, a token or a certificate.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Check the Common mistakes list below this diagram for what usually goes wrong here.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n2\">\u003cdt>The parts of a decision \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Think of four fields. The principal is who asks (a person, a service, a pipeline).\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Check the Common mistakes list below this diagram for what usually goes wrong here.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n3\">\u003cdt>Humans and workloads \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Network edge\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> People sign in through a central identity provider, ideally with single sign-on and MFA, and receive short-lived sessions.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Long-lived keys in code or files.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Using the root or owner account for daily work; creating one shared login for a team; giving administrator rights &quot;just to get it working&quot;; leaving keys with no expiry; and never removing access when someone changes team. Treat identities like inventory: know who has what, why, and when it ends.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> A pipeline needs to upload files to one storage bucket. Which identity is the best choice?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> A dedicated workload identity with short-lived credentials and permission only to write to that one bucket.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Specific principal, specific action, specific resource.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-key-in-the-repository\">Incident: The key in the repository\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A test key for a deploy pipeline was committed to a repository and found by a scanner three days later.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"git log --oneline -1 -- deploy/.env\naws iam list-access-keys --user-name ci-deploy\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>git \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">log\u003c/span> --oneline -1 -- deploy/.env\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">3f9a1c2 add deploy env\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>aws iam list-access-keys --user-name ci-deploy\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">AccessKeyId: AKIA...EXAMPLE  Status: Active  CreateDate: 2024-03-02\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The key belonged to a user with broad permissions and no expiry. The team deactivated it, reviewed the audit log for use from unknown addresses, replaced it with a role that the pipeline assumes for short sessions, and added secret scanning before every commit lands.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes-2\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Using the root account for daily work\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Sharing one login between people\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Long-lived keys in code or files\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Skipping MFA for administrators\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"authentication-and-authorization","text":"Authentication and authorization"},{"id":"the-parts-of-a-decision","text":"The parts of a decision"},{"id":"humans-and-workloads","text":"Humans and workloads"},{"id":"how-the-parts-fit-together","text":"How the parts fit together"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"},{"id":"incident-the-key-in-the-repository","text":"Incident: The key in the repository"},{"id":"common-mistakes-2","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"AWS IAM user guide","u":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/introduction.html"},{"t":"Azure RBAC overview","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/role-based-access-control/overview"}],"text":"Every cloud action is a request: someone or something asks to do an action on a resource. The cloud checks identity, then permissions, then conditions, and answers allow or deny. Almost every security problem in the cloud is a mistake in that chain: the wrong identity, too many permissions, or a condition nobody checked. This lesson gives you the vocabulary before the provider-specific lessons. What you will learn Separate authentication from authorization Name the parts of an access decision Tell human identities from workload identities Spot the most common identity mistakes Authentication and authorization Authentication answers \"who are you?\" with a password, a hardware key, a token or a certificate. Authorization answers \"what may you do?\" by comparing the request with policies. A system can authenticate you correctly and still wrongly authorize you, and the second mistake is the common one. Multi-factor authentication protects the first step; least privilege protects the second. The parts of a decision Think of four fields. The principal is who asks (a person, a service, a pipeline). The action is what they want (read an object, start a server). The resource is the thing acted on (one bucket, one subscription). The conditions narrow it further (only from this network, only today, only with MFA). A policy is a statement that allows or denies a combination of these. Most clouds start from deny by default: with no matching allow, the answer is no. An explicit deny beats any allow. Humans and workloads People sign in through a central identity provider, ideally with single sign-on and MFA, and receive short-lived sessions. Workloads such as a virtual machine, a function or a Kubernetes pod need identities too. The safe pattern is an identity that the platform attaches to the workload, which gives it temporary credentials automatically. The unsafe pattern is a long-lived access key copied into a configuration file or a repository, where it is leaked, shared and never rotated. How the parts fit together The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it. How Identities, principals and permissions fits together Flow in order: 1, Authentication and authorization to The parts of a decision; 2, The parts of a decision to Humans and workloads. Security Step Network edge Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Authentication and authorization Security What it does: Authentication answers \"who are you?\" with a password, a hardware key, a token or a certificate. If it fails or is weak: Check the Common mistakes list below this diagram for what usually goes wrong here. The parts of a decision Step What it does: Think of four fields. The principal is who asks (a person, a service, a pipeline). If it fails or is weak: Check the Common mistakes list below this diagram for what usually goes wrong here. Humans and workloads Network edge What it does: People sign in through a central identity provider, ideally with single sign-on and MFA, and receive short-lived sessions. If it fails or is weak: Long-lived keys in code or files. Common mistakes Using the root or owner account for daily work; creating one shared login for a team; giving administrator rights \"just to get it working\"; leaving keys with no expiry; and never removing access when someone changes team. Treat identities like inventory: know who has what, why, and when it ends. Predict first. A pipeline needs to upload files to one storage bucket. Which identity is the best choice? Your guess Answer: A dedicated workload identity with short-lived credentials and permission only to write to that one bucket. Specific principal, specific action, specific resource. Incident: The key in the repository Incident A test key for a deploy pipeline was committed to a repository and found by a scanner three da"},"cloud-iam/aws-iam-policies-roles-and-assume-role":{"html":"\u003cp>AWS Identity and Access Management is the permission system for the whole account. You will meet users, groups, roles and policies. The most important idea is the role: an identity with no permanent password or key that a trusted principal can assume for a short session. If you remember one rule, make it this one: people and workloads should assume roles, not hold long-lived keys.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Read a JSON policy\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Know the difference between identity-based and resource-based policies\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Use roles and assume-role for workloads and cross-account access\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Understand how boundaries and service control policies limit permissions\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"reading-a-policy\">Reading a policy\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A policy lists statements. Each has an effect (Allow or Deny), actions, resources and optional conditions.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"json\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"{\n  &quot;Version&quot;: &quot;2012-10-17&quot;,\n  &quot;Statement&quot;: [{\n    &quot;Effect&quot;: &quot;Allow&quot;,\n    &quot;Action&quot;: [&quot;s3:GetObject&quot;],\n    &quot;Resource&quot;: &quot;arn:aws:s3:::reports-prod/summaries/*&quot;\n  }]\n}\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;Version&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;2012-10-17&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;Statement&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">[\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;Effect&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Allow&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;Action&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">[\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;s3:GetObject&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">]\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;Resource&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;arn:aws:s3:::reports-prod/summaries/*&quot;\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">]\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>This allows reading objects under one prefix of one bucket and nothing else. A wildcard such as \u003ccode>&quot;Action&quot;: &quot;*&quot;\u003c/code> with \u003ccode>&quot;Resource&quot;: &quot;*&quot;\u003c/code> is administrator access.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"two-kinds-of-policy\">Two kinds of policy\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>An \u003cstrong>identity-based policy\u003c/strong> is attached to a user, group or role and says what that identity may do. A \u003cstrong>resource-based policy\u003c/strong> is attached to a resource, such as a bucket or a queue, and says who may use it, including principals from other accounts. A request is allowed when the right policies allow it and nothing denies it.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"roles-and-assume-role\">Roles and assume-role\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A role has two parts: a \u003cstrong>trust policy\u003c/strong> saying who may assume it, and \u003cstrong>permission policies\u003c/strong> saying what it can do. A caller asks the Security Token Service to assume the role and receives temporary credentials, usually valid for an hour or less. Compute services attach roles directly: an instance profile for a virtual machine, an execution role for a function, a task role for a container. Cross-account access works the same way: the target account trusts a role in the source account.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"limits-above-policies\">Limits above policies\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A \u003cstrong>permissions boundary\u003c/strong> caps what an identity can ever be granted. A \u003cstrong>service control policy\u003c/strong> in AWS Organizations caps what whole accounts can do, even for their administrators. They never grant by themselves; they only restrict. Use them for guardrails such as &quot;no one may disable audit logging&quot; or &quot;only approved regions&quot;.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> A role has an allow for s3:* but an organization policy denies deleting objects. Can the role delete?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> No, because the organization policy limits the account and an explicit restriction wins.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Guardrails restrict what policies can allow.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-the-parts-fit-together\">How the parts fit together\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"n1&gt;n2;n2&gt;n3;n3&gt;n4\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">How AWS IAM: policies, roles and assume-role fits together\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n1\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Reading a policy\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n2\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Two kinds of policy\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n3\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Roles and assume-role\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n4\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Limits above policies\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Reading a policy to Two kinds of policy; 2, Two kinds of policy to Roles and assume-role; 3, Roles and assume-role to Limits above policies.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-security\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Security\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n1\">\u003cdt>Reading a policy \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> A policy lists statements. Each has an effect (Allow or Deny), actions, resources and optional conditions.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Using IAM users with keys for workloads.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n2\">\u003cdt>Two kinds of policy \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> An identity-based policy is attached to a user, group or role and says what that identity may do.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Forgetting resource-based policies when debugging.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n3\">\u003cdt>Roles and assume-role \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> A role has two parts: a trust policy saying who may assume it, and permission policies saying what it can do.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Trust policies that allow any principal.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n4\">\u003cdt>Limits above policies \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> A permissions boundary caps what an identity can ever be granted.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> They never grant by themselves; they only restrict.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-wildcard-that-opened-the-bucket\">Incident: The wildcard that opened the bucket\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A developer fixed an access error by granting s3:* on all buckets to a build role.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"aws iam get-role-policy --role-name build --policy-name tmp\naws iam simulate-principal-policy --policy-source-arn arn:aws:iam::123456789012:role/build --action-names s3:DeleteObject --resource-arns arn:aws:s3:::customer-exports/a.csv\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>aws iam get-role-policy --role-name build --policy-name tmp\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">&quot;Action&quot;: &quot;s3:*&quot;, &quot;Resource&quot;: &quot;*&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>aws iam simulate-principal-policy --policy-source-arn arn:aws:iam::123456789012:role/build --action-names s3:DeleteObject --resource-arns arn:aws:s3:::customer-exports/a.csv\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">EvalDecision: allowed\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The simulator showed the build role could delete customer exports. The team replaced the wildcard with the one bucket and prefix the build needs, added a boundary that denies deletes on data buckets, and put policy checks into code review.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Granting &quot;*&quot; to make an error disappear\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Using IAM users with keys for workloads\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Trust policies that allow any principal\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Forgetting resource-based policies when debugging\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"reading-a-policy","text":"Reading a policy"},{"id":"two-kinds-of-policy","text":"Two kinds of policy"},{"id":"roles-and-assume-role","text":"Roles and assume-role"},{"id":"limits-above-policies","text":"Limits above policies"},{"id":"how-the-parts-fit-together","text":"How the parts fit together"},{"id":"incident-the-wildcard-that-opened-the-bucket","text":"Incident: The wildcard that opened the bucket"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"IAM policy evaluation logic","u":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/reference_policies_evaluation-logic.html"},{"t":"AWS IAM user guide","u":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/introduction.html"}],"text":"AWS Identity and Access Management is the permission system for the whole account. You will meet users, groups, roles and policies. The most important idea is the role: an identity with no permanent password or key that a trusted principal can assume for a short session. If you remember one rule, make it this one: people and workloads should assume roles, not hold long-lived keys. What you will learn Read a JSON policy Know the difference between identity-based and resource-based policies Use roles and assume-role for workloads and cross-account access Understand how boundaries and service control policies limit permissions Reading a policy A policy lists statements. Each has an effect (Allow or Deny), actions, resources and optional conditions. { \"Version\" : \"2012-10-17\" , \"Statement\" : [ { \"Effect\" : \"Allow\" , \"Action\" : [ \"s3:GetObject\" ] , \"Resource\" : \"arn:aws:s3:::reports-prod/summaries/*\" } ] } This allows reading objects under one prefix of one bucket and nothing else. A wildcard such as \"Action\": \"*\" with \"Resource\": \"*\" is administrator access. Two kinds of policy An identity-based policy is attached to a user, group or role and says what that identity may do. A resource-based policy is attached to a resource, such as a bucket or a queue, and says who may use it, including principals from other accounts. A request is allowed when the right policies allow it and nothing denies it. Roles and assume-role A role has two parts: a trust policy saying who may assume it, and permission policies saying what it can do. A caller asks the Security Token Service to assume the role and receives temporary credentials, usually valid for an hour or less. Compute services attach roles directly: an instance profile for a virtual machine, an execution role for a function, a task role for a container. Cross-account access works the same way: the target account trusts a role in the source account. Limits above policies A permissions boundary caps what an identity can ever be granted. A service control policy in AWS Organizations caps what whole accounts can do, even for their administrators. They never grant by themselves; they only restrict. Use them for guardrails such as \"no one may disable audit logging\" or \"only approved regions\". Predict first. A role has an allow for s3:* but an organization policy denies deleting objects. Can the role delete? Your guess Answer: No, because the organization policy limits the account and an explicit restriction wins. Guardrails restrict what policies can allow. How the parts fit together The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it. How AWS IAM: policies, roles and assume-role fits together Flow in order: 1, Reading a policy to Two kinds of policy; 2, Two kinds of policy to Roles and assume-role; 3, Roles and assume-role to Limits above policies. Security Step Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Reading a policy Security What it does: A policy lists statements. Each has an effect (Allow or Deny), actions, resources and optional conditions. If it fails or is weak: Using IAM users with keys for workloads. Two kinds of policy Security What it does: An identity-based policy is attached to a user, group or role and says what that identity may do. If it fails or is weak: Forgetting resource-based policies when debugging. Roles and assume-role Step What it does: A role has two parts: a trust policy saying who may assume it, and permission policies saying what it can do. If it fails or is weak: Trust policies that allow any principal. Limits above policies Security What it does: A permissions boundary caps what an identity can ever be granted. If it fails or is weak: They never grant by themselves; they only restrict. Incident: The wildcard that opened the bucket Incident A developer fixed an access error by gra"},"cloud-iam/azure-entra-id-rbac-and-managed-identities":{"html":"\u003cp>Azure splits identity from access to resources. Microsoft Entra ID is the directory that holds users, groups and applications. Azure role-based access control decides what those identities can do to subscriptions, resource groups and resources. Learning to keep the two ideas apart makes most Azure access questions easy to answer.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Explain Entra ID and Azure RBAC\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Choose the right scope for a role assignment\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Use managed identities instead of secrets\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Use conditional access and privileged access sensibly\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"directory-and-resources\">Directory and resources\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Entra ID authenticates people and applications and issues tokens. It also has directory roles, such as managing users, which are separate from Azure resource roles. Azure RBAC governs resources: a \u003cstrong>role definition\u003c/strong> lists allowed actions, a \u003cstrong>scope\u003c/strong> says where it applies, and a \u003cstrong>role assignment\u003c/strong> ties a principal to a role at a scope.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"scopes-and-inheritance\">Scopes and inheritance\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Scopes form a hierarchy: management group, subscription, resource group, resource. An assignment at a higher scope is inherited by everything below it. Assign roles at the lowest scope that works. Giving Contributor on a subscription to fix one resource group problem grants access to everything in it.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"built-in-roles\">Built-in roles\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Start with built-in roles such as Reader, Contributor and Owner, and narrower ones like Storage Blob Data Reader. Owner can also assign roles, so reserve it. Note that control-plane roles and data-plane roles differ: being Contributor on a storage account does not automatically allow reading blob data.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"managed-identities\">Managed identities\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A \u003cstrong>managed identity\u003c/strong> is an identity that Azure creates for a resource, such as a virtual machine, app service or function, and manages for you. The application requests a token from a local endpoint and uses it to reach other services, with no secret to store or rotate. A system-assigned identity lives and dies with its resource; a user-assigned identity is created separately and can be shared.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"protecting-sign-ins\">Protecting sign-ins\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Conditional access policies require MFA, compliant devices or trusted locations. Privileged Identity Management gives administrators just-in-time, time-limited elevation with approval and logs, instead of standing permanent rights.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> An app needs to read secrets from a key vault. Which pattern avoids storing a credential?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> Give the app a managed identity and assign it a narrow role on that key vault.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The platform supplies the token.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-the-parts-fit-together\">How the parts fit together\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"n1&gt;n2;n2&gt;n3;n3&gt;n4;n4&gt;n5\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">How Azure: Entra ID, RBAC and managed identities fits together\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"10.0\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"30.0\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"30.0\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"70.0\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"70.0\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"90.0\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n1\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:10.0%;top:25.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Directory and resources\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n2\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:30.0%;top:75.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Scopes and inheritance\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n3\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:25.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Built-in roles\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n4\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:70.0%;top:75.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Managed identities\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n5\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:90.0%;top:25.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Protecting sign-ins\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Directory and resources to Scopes and inheritance; 2, Scopes and inheritance to Built-in roles; 3, Built-in roles to Managed identities; 4, Managed identities to Protecting sign-ins.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n1\">\u003cdt>Directory and resources \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Entra ID authenticates people and applications and issues tokens.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Confusing directory roles with resource roles.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n2\">\u003cdt>Scopes and inheritance \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Scopes form a hierarchy: management group, subscription, resource group, resource.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Assigning roles at the subscription level by default.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n3\">\u003cdt>Built-in roles \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Start with built-in roles such as Reader, Contributor and Owner, and narrower ones like Storage Blob Data Reader.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Leaving permanent Owner rights.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n4\">\u003cdt>Managed identities \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> A managed identity is an identity that Azure creates for a resource, such as a virtual machine, app service or function, and manages for you.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Storing client secrets when a managed identity works.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n5\">\u003cdt>Protecting sign-ins \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Conditional access policies require MFA, compliant devices or trusted locations.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Check the Common mistakes list below this diagram for what usually goes wrong here.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-contributor-at-the-subscription\">Incident: Contributor at the subscription\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A contractor needed to restart one web app and was made Contributor on the whole subscription.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"az role assignment list --assignee contractor@example.com --all -o table\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>az role assignment list --assignee contractor@example.com --all -o table\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Role         Scope\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Contributor  /subscriptions/0000-1111\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The contractor could change every resource, including production databases. The team removed the assignment, created a custom role allowing only restart on that app, assigned it at the resource scope, and enabled time-limited elevation for similar needs.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Assigning roles at the subscription level by default\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Confusing directory roles with resource roles\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Storing client secrets when a managed identity works\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Leaving permanent Owner rights\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"directory-and-resources","text":"Directory and resources"},{"id":"scopes-and-inheritance","text":"Scopes and inheritance"},{"id":"built-in-roles","text":"Built-in roles"},{"id":"managed-identities","text":"Managed identities"},{"id":"protecting-sign-ins","text":"Protecting sign-ins"},{"id":"how-the-parts-fit-together","text":"How the parts fit together"},{"id":"incident-contributor-at-the-subscription","text":"Incident: Contributor at the subscription"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Azure RBAC overview","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/role-based-access-control/overview"},{"t":"Managed identities for Azure resources","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/entra/identity/managed-identities-azure-resources/overview"}],"text":"Azure splits identity from access to resources. Microsoft Entra ID is the directory that holds users, groups and applications. Azure role-based access control decides what those identities can do to subscriptions, resource groups and resources. Learning to keep the two ideas apart makes most Azure access questions easy to answer. What you will learn Explain Entra ID and Azure RBAC Choose the right scope for a role assignment Use managed identities instead of secrets Use conditional access and privileged access sensibly Directory and resources Entra ID authenticates people and applications and issues tokens. It also has directory roles, such as managing users, which are separate from Azure resource roles. Azure RBAC governs resources: a role definition lists allowed actions, a scope says where it applies, and a role assignment ties a principal to a role at a scope. Scopes and inheritance Scopes form a hierarchy: management group, subscription, resource group, resource. An assignment at a higher scope is inherited by everything below it. Assign roles at the lowest scope that works. Giving Contributor on a subscription to fix one resource group problem grants access to everything in it. Built-in roles Start with built-in roles such as Reader, Contributor and Owner, and narrower ones like Storage Blob Data Reader. Owner can also assign roles, so reserve it. Note that control-plane roles and data-plane roles differ: being Contributor on a storage account does not automatically allow reading blob data. Managed identities A managed identity is an identity that Azure creates for a resource, such as a virtual machine, app service or function, and manages for you. The application requests a token from a local endpoint and uses it to reach other services, with no secret to store or rotate. A system-assigned identity lives and dies with its resource; a user-assigned identity is created separately and can be shared. Protecting sign-ins Conditional access policies require MFA, compliant devices or trusted locations. Privileged Identity Management gives administrators just-in-time, time-limited elevation with approval and logs, instead of standing permanent rights. Predict first. An app needs to read secrets from a key vault. Which pattern avoids storing a credential? Your guess Answer: Give the app a managed identity and assign it a narrow role on that key vault. The platform supplies the token. How the parts fit together The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it. How Azure: Entra ID, RBAC and managed identities fits together Flow in order: 1, Directory and resources to Scopes and inheritance; 2, Scopes and inheritance to Built-in roles; 3, Built-in roles to Managed identities; 4, Managed identities to Protecting sign-ins. Step Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Directory and resources Step What it does: Entra ID authenticates people and applications and issues tokens. If it fails or is weak: Confusing directory roles with resource roles. Scopes and inheritance Step What it does: Scopes form a hierarchy: management group, subscription, resource group, resource. If it fails or is weak: Assigning roles at the subscription level by default. Built-in roles Step What it does: Start with built-in roles such as Reader, Contributor and Owner, and narrower ones like Storage Blob Data Reader. If it fails or is weak: Leaving permanent Owner rights. Managed identities Step What it does: A managed identity is an identity that Azure creates for a resource, such as a virtual machine, app service or function, and manages for you. If it fails or is weak: Storing client secrets when a managed identity works. Protecting sign-ins Step What it does: Conditional access policies require MFA, compliant devices or trusted locations. If it fails or is weak: Check the"},"cloud-iam/least-privilege-and-access-reviews":{"html":"\u003cp>Least privilege means every identity has only the access its job needs, for only as long as needed. Nobody gets it perfect on day one, and permissions grow over time. The practical method is to start narrow, measure what is really used, remove the rest, and review on a schedule so access does not quietly accumulate.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Describe least privilege in practice\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Use access analysis tools to find unused permissions\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Design roles around jobs, not people\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Run a simple access review\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"start-narrow-then-measure\">Start narrow, then measure\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Begin with the permissions you know are needed. When a request is denied, read the exact action and resource in the error and add only that. In the other direction, look at what existing identities actually use. AWS offers access analysis showing last-accessed services and can generate a policy from recorded activity; Azure and others show similar usage data. Remove what has not been used for a period such as 90 days, after checking with the owner.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"roles-for-jobs\">Roles for jobs\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Create roles for functions (read-only auditor, deployer for one application, database operator) and assign people to them through groups. This avoids a unique set of permissions per person that nobody can review. Separate duties: the person who approves a change should not be the only one who can make and deploy it.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"temporary-and-conditional-access\">Temporary and conditional access\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Prefer time-limited access for rare tasks, with approval and automatic expiry. Add conditions: require MFA, restrict to expected networks or regions, require a ticket reference in the session name. Break-glass accounts for emergencies should exist, be stored safely, be monitored, and trigger an alert when used.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"access-reviews\">Access reviews\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Every quarter, list who has privileged access and ask the owner to confirm. Check service accounts, third-party integrations and old contractors. Record the date, the reviewer and the changes made. A review that finds nothing to remove for a year probably is not looking at real data.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> A role has 400 permitted actions, but the logs show it used 12 in 90 days. What do you do?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> Replace the policy with one that grants those 12 actions after confirming with the owner, and watch for denials.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Remove unused access.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-the-parts-fit-together\">How the parts fit together\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"n1&gt;n2;n2&gt;n3;n3&gt;n4\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">How Least privilege and access reviews fits together\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n1\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Start narrow, then measure\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n2\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Roles for jobs\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n3\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Temporary and conditional access\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n4\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Access reviews\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Start narrow, then measure to Roles for jobs; 2, Roles for jobs to Temporary and conditional access; 3, Temporary and conditional access to Access reviews.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-security\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Security\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n1\">\u003cdt>Start narrow, then measure \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Begin with the permissions you know are needed.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Reviews without usage data.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n2\">\u003cdt>Roles for jobs \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Create roles for functions (read-only auditor, deployer for one application, database operator) and assign people to them through groups.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Check the Common mistakes list below this diagram for what usually goes wrong here.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n3\">\u003cdt>Temporary and conditional access \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Prefer time-limited access for rare tasks, with approval and automatic expiry.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Granting broad rights because tuning takes time.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n4\">\u003cdt>Access reviews \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Every quarter, list who has privileged access and ask the owner to confirm.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> No owner for service accounts.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-nobody-remembers-why\">Incident: Nobody remembers why\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A security review found 31 service accounts with administrator rights and no owner.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"aws iam generate-service-last-accessed-details --arn arn:aws:iam::123456789012:role/legacy-batch\naws iam get-service-last-accessed-details --job-id 1234\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>aws iam generate-service-last-accessed-details --arn arn:aws:iam::123456789012:role/legacy-batch\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>aws iam get-service-last-accessed-details --job-id 1234\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">ServiceNamespace: s3  LastAuthenticated: 2023-11-02\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">ServiceNamespace: ec2  LastAuthenticated: -\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The report showed the role touched only one service, long ago. The team assigned owners, disabled unused accounts, replaced administrator rights with specific policies, and scheduled a quarterly review with a record of each decision.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Granting broad rights because tuning takes time\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No owner for service accounts\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Reviews without usage data\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No emergency access plan\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"start-narrow-then-measure","text":"Start narrow, then measure"},{"id":"roles-for-jobs","text":"Roles for jobs"},{"id":"temporary-and-conditional-access","text":"Temporary and conditional access"},{"id":"access-reviews","text":"Access reviews"},{"id":"how-the-parts-fit-together","text":"How the parts fit together"},{"id":"incident-nobody-remembers-why","text":"Incident: Nobody remembers why"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"AWS Well-Architected: Security pillar","u":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/security-pillar/welcome.html"},{"t":"Azure RBAC overview","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/role-based-access-control/overview"}],"text":"Least privilege means every identity has only the access its job needs, for only as long as needed. Nobody gets it perfect on day one, and permissions grow over time. The practical method is to start narrow, measure what is really used, remove the rest, and review on a schedule so access does not quietly accumulate. What you will learn Describe least privilege in practice Use access analysis tools to find unused permissions Design roles around jobs, not people Run a simple access review Start narrow, then measure Begin with the permissions you know are needed. When a request is denied, read the exact action and resource in the error and add only that. In the other direction, look at what existing identities actually use. AWS offers access analysis showing last-accessed services and can generate a policy from recorded activity; Azure and others show similar usage data. Remove what has not been used for a period such as 90 days, after checking with the owner. Roles for jobs Create roles for functions (read-only auditor, deployer for one application, database operator) and assign people to them through groups. This avoids a unique set of permissions per person that nobody can review. Separate duties: the person who approves a change should not be the only one who can make and deploy it. Temporary and conditional access Prefer time-limited access for rare tasks, with approval and automatic expiry. Add conditions: require MFA, restrict to expected networks or regions, require a ticket reference in the session name. Break-glass accounts for emergencies should exist, be stored safely, be monitored, and trigger an alert when used. Access reviews Every quarter, list who has privileged access and ask the owner to confirm. Check service accounts, third-party integrations and old contractors. Record the date, the reviewer and the changes made. A review that finds nothing to remove for a year probably is not looking at real data. Predict first. A role has 400 permitted actions, but the logs show it used 12 in 90 days. What do you do? Your guess Answer: Replace the policy with one that grants those 12 actions after confirming with the owner, and watch for denials. Remove unused access. How the parts fit together The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it. How Least privilege and access reviews fits together Flow in order: 1, Start narrow, then measure to Roles for jobs; 2, Roles for jobs to Temporary and conditional access; 3, Temporary and conditional access to Access reviews. Step Security Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Start narrow, then measure Step What it does: Begin with the permissions you know are needed. If it fails or is weak: Reviews without usage data. Roles for jobs Step What it does: Create roles for functions (read-only auditor, deployer for one application, database operator) and assign people to them through groups. If it fails or is weak: Check the Common mistakes list below this diagram for what usually goes wrong here. Temporary and conditional access Security What it does: Prefer time-limited access for rare tasks, with approval and automatic expiry. If it fails or is weak: Granting broad rights because tuning takes time. Access reviews Security What it does: Every quarter, list who has privileged access and ask the owner to confirm. If it fails or is weak: No owner for service accounts. Incident: Nobody remembers why Incident A security review found 31 service accounts with administrator rights and no owner. $ aws iam generate-service-last-accessed-details --arn arn:aws:iam::123456789012:role/legacy-batch $ aws iam get-service-last-accessed-details --job-id 1234 ServiceNamespace: s3 LastAuthenticated: 2023-11-02 ServiceNamespace: ec2 LastAuthenticated: - The report showed the role touched only one service, long ago."},"cloud-iam/secrets-and-encryption-with-kms-and-key-vaults":{"html":"\u003cp>Passwords, tokens and keys must live somewhere. The wrong places are source code, container images, chat messages and shared documents. The right place is a managed secrets store, with access controlled by identity, every read logged, and rotation built in. Encryption keys are a related but different subject, and cloud key management services keep them safe so you never handle raw key material.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Choose a place for secrets and keep them out of code\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Explain encryption at rest and in transit\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Understand keys, envelope encryption and key policies\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Rotate secrets safely\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"secrets-stores\">Secrets stores\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>AWS Secrets Manager, AWS Systems Manager Parameter Store and Azure Key Vault store secrets and return them to authorised identities at run time. The application fetches the value with its workload identity, holds it in memory and never writes it to disk or logs. Access is a permission like any other, so a pipeline may read only its own secrets. Every read appears in the audit log.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"keys-and-envelope-encryption\">Keys and envelope encryption\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A key management service (AWS KMS, Azure Key Vault keys, Google Cloud KMS) creates keys and performs encryption without exposing the key. For large data, \u003cstrong>envelope encryption\u003c/strong> is used: a data key encrypts the data, and the master key in KMS encrypts the data key. Storing the encrypted data key beside the data keeps performance high and control central. Key policies decide who can use and who can manage a key; separate those two powers.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"at-rest-and-in-transit\">At rest and in transit\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Encrypt storage at rest by default for disks, databases and object storage, using managed or customer-managed keys when rules demand control. Encrypt traffic in transit with TLS and reject plain connections. Encryption does not replace access control: anyone with permission to decrypt sees the plain data.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"rotation\">Rotation\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Rotate credentials on a schedule and immediately after exposure. Safe rotation supports two valid versions for a while: create the new, switch consumers, then retire the old. Managed rotation for database passwords can do this automatically. Prefer short-lived tokens, which make rotation a non-event.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> A database password is needed by a service. Where should it live and how should the service get it?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> In a secrets manager; the service fetches it at start-up using its workload identity.\u003c/p>\u003cp>No password in code or images.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-the-parts-fit-together\">How the parts fit together\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"n1&gt;n2;n2&gt;n3;n3&gt;n4\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">How Secrets and encryption with KMS and key vaults fits together\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n1\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Secrets stores\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n2\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Keys and envelope encryption\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n3\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">At rest and in transit\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n4\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Rotation\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Secrets stores to Keys and envelope encryption; 2, Keys and envelope encryption to At rest and in transit; 3, At rest and in transit to Rotation.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-security\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Security\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n1\">\u003cdt>Secrets stores \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> AWS Secrets Manager, AWS Systems Manager Parameter Store and Azure Key Vault store secrets and return them to authorised identities at run time.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Secrets in code, images or logs.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n2\">\u003cdt>Keys and envelope encryption \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> A key management service (AWS KMS, Azure Key Vault keys, Google Cloud KMS) creates keys and performs encryption without exposing the key.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Keys and envelope encryption A key management service (AWS KMS, Azure Key Vault keys, Google Cloud KMS) creates keys and performs...\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n3\">\u003cdt>At rest and in transit \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Encrypt storage at rest by default for disks, databases and object storage, using managed or customer-managed keys when rules demand control.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Giving everyone permission to decrypt.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n4\">\u003cdt>Rotation \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Rotate credentials on a schedule and immediately after exposure.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> No rotation plan.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-environment-variable-in-the-image\">Incident: The environment variable in the image\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A container image published to a registry contained a database password in an environment variable.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"docker history --no-trunc shop-api:2.3 | grep DB_PASSWORD\ntrivy image --scanners secret shop-api:2.3\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>docker \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">history\u003c/span> --no-trunc shop-api:2.3 | grep DB_PASSWORD\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">ENV DB_PASSWORD=Sup3rS3cret!\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>trivy image --scanners secret shop-api:2.3\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">HIGH: secret in layer 4\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Anyone who could pull the image could read the password. The team rotated the password, rebuilt the image without it, moved the value to the secrets manager with access limited to the service role, and added secret scanning to the build.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Secrets in code, images or logs\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>One key for everything\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No rotation plan\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Giving everyone permission to decrypt\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"secrets-stores","text":"Secrets stores"},{"id":"keys-and-envelope-encryption","text":"Keys and envelope encryption"},{"id":"at-rest-and-in-transit","text":"At rest and in transit"},{"id":"rotation","text":"Rotation"},{"id":"how-the-parts-fit-together","text":"How the parts fit together"},{"id":"incident-the-environment-variable-in-the-image","text":"Incident: The environment variable in the image"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"AWS Well-Architected: Security pillar","u":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/security-pillar/welcome.html"},{"t":"Managed identities for Azure resources","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/entra/identity/managed-identities-azure-resources/overview"}],"text":"Passwords, tokens and keys must live somewhere. The wrong places are source code, container images, chat messages and shared documents. The right place is a managed secrets store, with access controlled by identity, every read logged, and rotation built in. Encryption keys are a related but different subject, and cloud key management services keep them safe so you never handle raw key material. What you will learn Choose a place for secrets and keep them out of code Explain encryption at rest and in transit Understand keys, envelope encryption and key policies Rotate secrets safely Secrets stores AWS Secrets Manager, AWS Systems Manager Parameter Store and Azure Key Vault store secrets and return them to authorised identities at run time. The application fetches the value with its workload identity, holds it in memory and never writes it to disk or logs. Access is a permission like any other, so a pipeline may read only its own secrets. Every read appears in the audit log. Keys and envelope encryption A key management service (AWS KMS, Azure Key Vault keys, Google Cloud KMS) creates keys and performs encryption without exposing the key. For large data, envelope encryption is used: a data key encrypts the data, and the master key in KMS encrypts the data key. Storing the encrypted data key beside the data keeps performance high and control central. Key policies decide who can use and who can manage a key; separate those two powers. At rest and in transit Encrypt storage at rest by default for disks, databases and object storage, using managed or customer-managed keys when rules demand control. Encrypt traffic in transit with TLS and reject plain connections. Encryption does not replace access control: anyone with permission to decrypt sees the plain data. Rotation Rotate credentials on a schedule and immediately after exposure. Safe rotation supports two valid versions for a while: create the new, switch consumers, then retire the old. Managed rotation for database passwords can do this automatically. Prefer short-lived tokens, which make rotation a non-event. Predict first. A database password is needed by a service. Where should it live and how should the service get it? Your guess Answer: In a secrets manager; the service fetches it at start-up using its workload identity. No password in code or images. How the parts fit together The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it. How Secrets and encryption with KMS and key vaults fits together Flow in order: 1, Secrets stores to Keys and envelope encryption; 2, Keys and envelope encryption to At rest and in transit; 3, At rest and in transit to Rotation. Security Step Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Secrets stores Security What it does: AWS Secrets Manager, AWS Systems Manager Parameter Store and Azure Key Vault store secrets and return them to authorised identities at run time. If it fails or is weak: Secrets in code, images or logs. Keys and envelope encryption Security What it does: A key management service (AWS KMS, Azure Key Vault keys, Google Cloud KMS) creates keys and performs encryption without exposing the key. If it fails or is weak: Keys and envelope encryption A key management service (AWS KMS, Azure Key Vault keys, Google Cloud KMS) creates keys and performs... At rest and in transit Step What it does: Encrypt storage at rest by default for disks, databases and object storage, using managed or customer-managed keys when rules demand control. If it fails or is weak: Giving everyone permission to decrypt. Rotation Step What it does: Rotate credentials on a schedule and immediately after exposure. If it fails or is weak: No rotation plan. Incident: The environment variable in the image Incident A container image published to a registry contained a database password in a"},"cloud-iam/network-boundaries-and-private-access":{"html":"\u003cp>Even perfect permissions are weaker when every resource is reachable from the internet. Network boundaries add a second layer: a service that cannot be reached cannot be attacked directly. Identity and network controls work together, and many cloud breaches began with a public storage bucket, an open database port or an unprotected management interface.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Use private subnets and security groups sensibly\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Reach cloud services privately with endpoints\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Close the common public exposures\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Combine network and identity controls\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"private-by-default\">Private by default\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Place application servers and databases in private subnets without public addresses. Put only load balancers and gateways in public subnets. Outbound traffic goes through a NAT gateway or a proxy you can monitor. In Azure, the same idea uses virtual networks, subnets and network security groups.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"security-groups-and-rules\">Security groups and rules\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A security group is a stateful firewall attached to a resource. Allow only the ports and sources needed, preferably referring to other security groups instead of wide address ranges. Be suspicious of \u003ccode>0.0.0.0/0\u003c/code> on anything other than a public web port, and never on SSH, RDP or database ports. Use session-based access (such as AWS Systems Manager Session Manager or Azure Bastion) instead of opening management ports.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"private-endpoints\">Private endpoints\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Cloud services such as object storage, key vaults and databases are normally reached through public addresses. \u003cstrong>Private endpoints\u003c/strong> (AWS PrivateLink and VPC endpoints, Azure Private Link) give the service a private address inside your network, so traffic never crosses the internet. Combine them with policies that deny access from anywhere else.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"public-exposure-checklist\">Public exposure checklist\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Block public access on storage accounts and buckets at the account level; check for public snapshots and images; review public IP addresses regularly; protect web applications with a firewall and rate limits; and scan for open ports from outside. Remember that a network rule is not authorisation: a service that is reachable still has to check identity.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> A database must be used only by the application servers. How do you configure it?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> Keep it in a private subnet and allow its port only from the application security group.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Reference the group, not an address range.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-the-parts-fit-together\">How the parts fit together\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"n1&gt;n2;n2&gt;n3;n3&gt;n4\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">How Network boundaries and private access fits together\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n1\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Private by default\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n2\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Security groups and rules\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n3\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Private endpoints\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n4\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Public exposure checklist\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Private by default to Security groups and rules; 2, Security groups and rules to Private endpoints; 3, Private endpoints to Public exposure checklist.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-security\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Security\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n1\">\u003cdt>Private by default \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Place application servers and databases in private subnets without public addresses.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Public IPs on databases.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n2\">\u003cdt>Security groups and rules \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> A security group is a stateful firewall attached to a resource.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Wide source ranges on management ports.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n3\">\u003cdt>Private endpoints \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Cloud services such as object storage, key vaults and databases are normally reached through public addresses.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Assuming a private network replaces authentication.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n4\">\u003cdt>Public exposure checklist \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Block public access on storage accounts and buckets at the account level; check for public snapshots and images; review public IP addresses regularly; protect web...\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> No account-level block on public storage.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-public-bucket\">Incident: The public bucket\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A storage bucket holding customer exports was reachable by anyone with the link.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"aws s3api get-public-access-block --bucket customer-exports\naws s3api get-bucket-policy-status --bucket customer-exports\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>aws s3api get-public-access-block --bucket customer-exports\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">An error occurred (NoSuchPublicAccessBlockConfiguration)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>aws s3api get-bucket-policy-status --bucket customer-exports\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">&quot;IsPublic&quot;: true\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The block on public access had never been enabled. The team turned it on for the whole account, replaced the sharing links with short-lived signed links, reviewed the access logs for outside reads, and added a rule that alerts on any public bucket.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Public IPs on databases\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Wide source ranges on management ports\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Assuming a private network replaces authentication\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No account-level block on public storage\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"private-by-default","text":"Private by default"},{"id":"security-groups-and-rules","text":"Security groups and rules"},{"id":"private-endpoints","text":"Private endpoints"},{"id":"public-exposure-checklist","text":"Public exposure checklist"},{"id":"how-the-parts-fit-together","text":"How the parts fit together"},{"id":"incident-the-public-bucket","text":"Incident: The public bucket"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"AWS Well-Architected: Security pillar","u":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/security-pillar/welcome.html"},{"t":"Azure RBAC overview","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/role-based-access-control/overview"}],"text":"Even perfect permissions are weaker when every resource is reachable from the internet. Network boundaries add a second layer: a service that cannot be reached cannot be attacked directly. Identity and network controls work together, and many cloud breaches began with a public storage bucket, an open database port or an unprotected management interface. What you will learn Use private subnets and security groups sensibly Reach cloud services privately with endpoints Close the common public exposures Combine network and identity controls Private by default Place application servers and databases in private subnets without public addresses. Put only load balancers and gateways in public subnets. Outbound traffic goes through a NAT gateway or a proxy you can monitor. In Azure, the same idea uses virtual networks, subnets and network security groups. Security groups and rules A security group is a stateful firewall attached to a resource. Allow only the ports and sources needed, preferably referring to other security groups instead of wide address ranges. Be suspicious of 0.0.0.0/0 on anything other than a public web port, and never on SSH, RDP or database ports. Use session-based access (such as AWS Systems Manager Session Manager or Azure Bastion) instead of opening management ports. Private endpoints Cloud services such as object storage, key vaults and databases are normally reached through public addresses. Private endpoints (AWS PrivateLink and VPC endpoints, Azure Private Link) give the service a private address inside your network, so traffic never crosses the internet. Combine them with policies that deny access from anywhere else. Public exposure checklist Block public access on storage accounts and buckets at the account level; check for public snapshots and images; review public IP addresses regularly; protect web applications with a firewall and rate limits; and scan for open ports from outside. Remember that a network rule is not authorisation: a service that is reachable still has to check identity. Predict first. A database must be used only by the application servers. How do you configure it? Your guess Answer: Keep it in a private subnet and allow its port only from the application security group. Reference the group, not an address range. How the parts fit together The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it. How Network boundaries and private access fits together Flow in order: 1, Private by default to Security groups and rules; 2, Security groups and rules to Private endpoints; 3, Private endpoints to Public exposure checklist. Step Security Observability Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Private by default Step What it does: Place application servers and databases in private subnets without public addresses. If it fails or is weak: Public IPs on databases. Security groups and rules Security What it does: A security group is a stateful firewall attached to a resource. If it fails or is weak: Wide source ranges on management ports. Private endpoints Step What it does: Cloud services such as object storage, key vaults and databases are normally reached through public addresses. If it fails or is weak: Assuming a private network replaces authentication. Public exposure checklist Observability What it does: Block public access on storage accounts and buckets at the account level; check for public snapshots and images; review public IP addresses regularly; protect web... If it fails or is weak: No account-level block on public storage. Incident: The public bucket Incident A storage bucket holding customer exports was reachable by anyone with the link. $ aws s3api get-public-access-block --bucket customer-exports An error occurred (NoSuchPublicAccessBlockConfiguration) $ aws s3api get-bucket-policy-status --bucket cus"},"cloud-iam/logging-detection-and-audit-trails":{"html":"\u003cp>You cannot investigate what you did not record. Cloud providers log administrative actions: AWS CloudTrail, the Azure Activity Log, and Google Cloud Audit Logs. These logs tell you who did what, when, from where. Collected, protected and watched, they turn a breach from an unknown loss into a time-ordered story.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Enable organisation-wide audit logging\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Read an audit record\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Protect logs from tampering\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Alert on high-risk actions\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-to-log\">What to log\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Management events (create, change, delete), data events for sensitive storage, sign-in logs from the identity provider, network flow logs, and application logs. Turn on audit logging in every account and region from day one, ideally centrally for the whole organisation.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"reading-a-record\">Reading a record\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>An audit record names the \u003cstrong>principal\u003c/strong>, the \u003cstrong>action\u003c/strong>, the \u003cstrong>resource\u003c/strong>, the \u003cstrong>source address\u003c/strong>, the \u003cstrong>time\u003c/strong> and whether it succeeded.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"json\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"{\n  &quot;eventTime&quot;: &quot;2024-05-14T02:11:09Z&quot;,\n  &quot;eventName&quot;: &quot;DeleteTrail&quot;,\n  &quot;userIdentity&quot;: { &quot;type&quot;: &quot;AssumedRole&quot;, &quot;arn&quot;: &quot;arn:aws:sts::123456789012:assumed-role/dev/anna&quot; },\n  &quot;sourceIPAddress&quot;: &quot;203.0.113.45&quot;,\n  &quot;errorCode&quot;: null\n}\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;eventTime&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;2024-05-14T02:11:09Z&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;eventName&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;DeleteTrail&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;userIdentity&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;type&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;AssumedRole&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;arn&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;arn:aws:sts::123456789012:assumed-role/dev/anna&quot;\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;sourceIPAddress&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;203.0.113.45&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;errorCode&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">null\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Deleting a trail at 02:11 from an unfamiliar address is exactly the kind of event to alert on.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"protect-the-logs\">Protect the logs\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Send logs to a separate account that few people can use. Make the storage append-only or write-once, enable integrity validation, and deny deletion. An attacker who can erase the log can hide the attack.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"detect-and-respond\">Detect and respond\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Start with a short list of alerts: use of the root account, a change to logging, new access keys for privileged users, a policy that grants administrator rights, a public resource, sign-ins from unusual places, and many denied requests. Use managed threat detection services as a baseline. Write a short runbook for each alert: who looks, what they check, and when to disable the identity.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> An alert shows someone disabled audit logging at night. What do you do first?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> Treat it as an incident: identify the principal, restore logging, review recent actions by that principal and disable the identity if it is not legitimate.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Logging changes are high risk.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-the-parts-fit-together\">How the parts fit together\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"n1&gt;n2;n2&gt;n3;n3&gt;n4\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">How Logging, detection and audit trails fits together\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n1\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">What to log\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n2\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Reading a record\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n3\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Protect the logs\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n4\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Detect and respond\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, What to log to Reading a record; 2, Reading a record to Protect the logs; 3, Protect the logs to Detect and respond.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n1\">\u003cdt>What to log \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Management events (create, change, delete), data events for sensitive storage, sign-in logs from the identity provider, network flow logs, and application logs.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Logs in the same account anyone can edit.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n2\">\u003cdt>Reading a record \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> An audit record names the principal, the action, the resource, the source address, the time and whether it succeeded.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> No alert on logging changes.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n3\">\u003cdt>Protect the logs \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Send logs to a separate account that few people can use.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Logging only in some regions.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n4\">\u003cdt>Detect and respond \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Start with a short list of alerts: use of the root account, a change to logging, new access keys for privileged users, a policy that grants administrator rights, a...\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Check the Common mistakes list below this diagram for what usually goes wrong here.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-logging-was-off\">Incident: Logging was off\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>During an investigation the team found a gap of nine days with no audit records.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"aws cloudtrail get-trail-status --name org-trail\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>aws cloudtrail get-trail-status --name org-trail\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">IsLogging: false\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">LatestDeliveryTime: 2024-05-05T01:02:13Z\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A cost-cutting change had stopped the trail. The team re-enabled it, added an organisation policy preventing anyone from stopping it, set an alert on the stop event, and sent copies to a locked-down log account.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Logging only in some regions\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Logs in the same account anyone can edit\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No alert on logging changes\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Collecting logs nobody reads\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"what-to-log","text":"What to log"},{"id":"reading-a-record","text":"Reading a record"},{"id":"protect-the-logs","text":"Protect the logs"},{"id":"detect-and-respond","text":"Detect and respond"},{"id":"how-the-parts-fit-together","text":"How the parts fit together"},{"id":"incident-logging-was-off","text":"Incident: Logging was off"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"AWS Well-Architected: Security pillar","u":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/security-pillar/welcome.html"},{"t":"Azure RBAC overview","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/role-based-access-control/overview"}],"text":"You cannot investigate what you did not record. Cloud providers log administrative actions: AWS CloudTrail, the Azure Activity Log, and Google Cloud Audit Logs. These logs tell you who did what, when, from where. Collected, protected and watched, they turn a breach from an unknown loss into a time-ordered story. What you will learn Enable organisation-wide audit logging Read an audit record Protect logs from tampering Alert on high-risk actions What to log Management events (create, change, delete), data events for sensitive storage, sign-in logs from the identity provider, network flow logs, and application logs. Turn on audit logging in every account and region from day one, ideally centrally for the whole organisation. Reading a record An audit record names the principal , the action , the resource , the source address , the time and whether it succeeded. { \"eventTime\" : \"2024-05-14T02:11:09Z\" , \"eventName\" : \"DeleteTrail\" , \"userIdentity\" : { \"type\" : \"AssumedRole\" , \"arn\" : \"arn:aws:sts::123456789012:assumed-role/dev/anna\" } , \"sourceIPAddress\" : \"203.0.113.45\" , \"errorCode\" : null } Deleting a trail at 02:11 from an unfamiliar address is exactly the kind of event to alert on. Protect the logs Send logs to a separate account that few people can use. Make the storage append-only or write-once, enable integrity validation, and deny deletion. An attacker who can erase the log can hide the attack. Detect and respond Start with a short list of alerts: use of the root account, a change to logging, new access keys for privileged users, a policy that grants administrator rights, a public resource, sign-ins from unusual places, and many denied requests. Use managed threat detection services as a baseline. Write a short runbook for each alert: who looks, what they check, and when to disable the identity. Predict first. An alert shows someone disabled audit logging at night. What do you do first? Your guess Answer: Treat it as an incident: identify the principal, restore logging, review recent actions by that principal and disable the identity if it is not legitimate. Logging changes are high risk. How the parts fit together The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it. How Logging, detection and audit trails fits together Flow in order: 1, What to log to Reading a record; 2, Reading a record to Protect the logs; 3, Protect the logs to Detect and respond. Observability Step Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. What to log Observability What it does: Management events (create, change, delete), data events for sensitive storage, sign-in logs from the identity provider, network flow logs, and application logs. If it fails or is weak: Logs in the same account anyone can edit. Reading a record Step What it does: An audit record names the principal, the action, the resource, the source address, the time and whether it succeeded. If it fails or is weak: No alert on logging changes. Protect the logs Observability What it does: Send logs to a separate account that few people can use. If it fails or is weak: Logging only in some regions. Detect and respond Step What it does: Start with a short list of alerts: use of the root account, a change to logging, new access keys for privileged users, a policy that grants administrator rights, a... If it fails or is weak: Check the Common mistakes list below this diagram for what usually goes wrong here. Incident: Logging was off Incident During an investigation the team found a gap of nine days with no audit records. $ aws cloudtrail get-trail-status --name org-trail IsLogging: false LatestDeliveryTime: 2024-05-05T01:02:13Z A cost-cutting change had stopped the trail. The team re-enabled it, added an organisation policy preventing anyone from stopping it, set an alert on the stop event, and sent copies to a"},"cloud-iam/debugging-access-denied-and-cloud-security-incidents":{"html":"\u003cp>Two situations test everything in this skill. In the first, a legitimate request fails with &quot;access denied&quot; and you need the narrowest fix. In the second, a credential has leaked and you need to contain damage quickly. Both reward a calm, ordered method.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Read an access-denied error\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Walk the evaluation chain\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Choose a narrow fix\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Respond to a leaked credential in order\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"read-the-error\">Read the error\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A good error names the principal, the action and the resource: &quot;User arn:...:role/build is not authorized to perform s3:PutObject on arn:aws:s3:::reports-prod/x&quot;. That is already the answer to three questions. Check that the principal is the one you expect, because many failures are caused by running as a different role than intended.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"walk-the-chain\">Walk the chain\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Check in order: is the right identity in use; does an identity policy allow the action on this resource; does a resource policy allow or deny it; does a boundary, organisation policy or deny statement restrict it; do conditions (network, MFA, tags, time) hold; and for data in storage or keys, does the key policy allow use. Policy simulators and the access analysis tools show which statement decided. Audit logs show the denied call with its error.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"fix-narrowly\">Fix narrowly\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Add the specific action and resource, not a wildcard. If the cause is a missing condition such as the request not coming through the private endpoint, fix the path rather than removing the condition. Record why the access is needed.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"suspected-credential-leak\">Suspected credential leak\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Act in this order: \u003cstrong>contain\u003c/strong> (deactivate the key or revoke the session, do not delete it yet); \u003cstrong>assess\u003c/strong> (audit logs for use of that credential, new users or keys, new resources, changed policies, copies of data); \u003cstrong>eradicate\u003c/strong> (remove attacker-created access, rotate related secrets); \u003cstrong>recover\u003c/strong> (replace with a role or short-lived credential); \u003cstrong>learn\u003c/strong> (how did it leak, what detection would have caught it sooner). Tell the right people early.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> A leaked key shows calls from an unknown address creating servers. What is your first action?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> Deactivate the key or revoke the session immediately, then review its activity and remove what it created.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Contain first.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-the-parts-fit-together\">How the parts fit together\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"n1&gt;n2;n2&gt;n3;n3&gt;n4\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">How Debugging access denied and cloud security... fits together\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n1\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Read the error\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n2\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Walk the chain\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n3\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Fix narrowly\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n4\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Suspected credential leak\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Read the error to Walk the chain; 2, Walk the chain to Fix narrowly; 3, Fix narrowly to Suspected credential leak.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n1\">\u003cdt>Read the error \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> A good error names the principal, the action and the resource: &quot;User arn:...:role/build is not authorized to perform s3:PutObject on arn:aws:s3:::reports-prod/x&quot;.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Check the Common mistakes list below this diagram for what usually goes wrong here.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n2\">\u003cdt>Walk the chain \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Check in order: is the right identity in use; does an identity policy allow the action on this resource; does a resource policy allow or deny it; does a boundary...\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Fixing access errors with wildcards.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n3\">\u003cdt>Fix narrowly \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Add the specific action and resource, not a wildcard.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Check the Common mistakes list below this diagram for what usually goes wrong here.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n4\">\u003cdt>Suspected credential leak \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Act in this order: contain (deactivate the key or revoke the session, do not delete it yet); assess (audit logs for use of that credential, new users or keys, new...\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Check the Common mistakes list below this diagram for what usually goes wrong here.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-crypto-servers-in-six-regions\">Incident: Crypto servers in six regions\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A cloud bill spiked because someone launched large servers in regions the team never used.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"aws cloudtrail lookup-events --lookup-attributes AttributeKey=AccessKeyId,AttributeValue=AKIA...EXAMPLE --max-results 3\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>aws cloudtrail lookup-events --lookup-attributes AttributeKey=AccessKeyId,AttributeValue=AKIA...EXAMPLE --max-results 3\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">RunInstances us-east-2  203.0.113.77\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">RunInstances ap-south-1 203.0.113.77\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">CreateAccessKey  203.0.113.77\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The key had been exposed in a public repository. The team disabled it, terminated the servers, deleted the attacker-created key, checked for changed policies, enabled organisation guardrails that deny unused regions, and moved the application to roles.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Deleting evidence before capturing it\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Fixing access errors with wildcards\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Rotating one secret while others stay exposed\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No guardrail against unused regions\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"read-the-error","text":"Read the error"},{"id":"walk-the-chain","text":"Walk the chain"},{"id":"fix-narrowly","text":"Fix narrowly"},{"id":"suspected-credential-leak","text":"Suspected credential leak"},{"id":"how-the-parts-fit-together","text":"How the parts fit together"},{"id":"incident-crypto-servers-in-six-regions","text":"Incident: Crypto servers in six regions"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"IAM policy evaluation logic","u":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/reference_policies_evaluation-logic.html"},{"t":"AWS Well-Architected: Security pillar","u":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/security-pillar/welcome.html"}],"text":"Two situations test everything in this skill. In the first, a legitimate request fails with \"access denied\" and you need the narrowest fix. In the second, a credential has leaked and you need to contain damage quickly. Both reward a calm, ordered method. What you will learn Read an access-denied error Walk the evaluation chain Choose a narrow fix Respond to a leaked credential in order Read the error A good error names the principal, the action and the resource: \"User arn:...:role/build is not authorized to perform s3:PutObject on arn:aws:s3:::reports-prod/x\". That is already the answer to three questions. Check that the principal is the one you expect, because many failures are caused by running as a different role than intended. Walk the chain Check in order: is the right identity in use; does an identity policy allow the action on this resource; does a resource policy allow or deny it; does a boundary, organisation policy or deny statement restrict it; do conditions (network, MFA, tags, time) hold; and for data in storage or keys, does the key policy allow use. Policy simulators and the access analysis tools show which statement decided. Audit logs show the denied call with its error. Fix narrowly Add the specific action and resource, not a wildcard. If the cause is a missing condition such as the request not coming through the private endpoint, fix the path rather than removing the condition. Record why the access is needed. Suspected credential leak Act in this order: contain (deactivate the key or revoke the session, do not delete it yet); assess (audit logs for use of that credential, new users or keys, new resources, changed policies, copies of data); eradicate (remove attacker-created access, rotate related secrets); recover (replace with a role or short-lived credential); learn (how did it leak, what detection would have caught it sooner). Tell the right people early. Predict first. A leaked key shows calls from an unknown address creating servers. What is your first action? Your guess Answer: Deactivate the key or revoke the session immediately, then review its activity and remove what it created. Contain first. How the parts fit together The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it. How Debugging access denied and cloud security... fits together Flow in order: 1, Read the error to Walk the chain; 2, Walk the chain to Fix narrowly; 3, Fix narrowly to Suspected credential leak. Step Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Read the error Step What it does: A good error names the principal, the action and the resource: \"User arn:...:role/build is not authorized to perform s3:PutObject on arn:aws:s3:::reports-prod/x\". If it fails or is weak: Check the Common mistakes list below this diagram for what usually goes wrong here. Walk the chain Step What it does: Check in order: is the right identity in use; does an identity policy allow the action on this resource; does a resource policy allow or deny it; does a boundary... If it fails or is weak: Fixing access errors with wildcards. Fix narrowly Step What it does: Add the specific action and resource, not a wildcard. If it fails or is weak: Check the Common mistakes list below this diagram for what usually goes wrong here. Suspected credential leak Step What it does: Act in this order: contain (deactivate the key or revoke the session, do not delete it yet); assess (audit logs for use of that credential, new users or keys, new... If it fails or is weak: Check the Common mistakes list below this diagram for what usually goes wrong here. Incident: Crypto servers in six regions Incident A cloud bill spiked because someone launched large servers in regions the team never used. $ aws cloudtrail lookup-events --lookup-attributes AttributeKey=AccessKeyId,AttributeValue=AKIA...EXAMPLE --max"},"cloud-iam/gcp-iam-and-service-accounts":{"html":"\u003cp>Google Cloud IAM has the same goals as other clouds but its own model: a resource hierarchy, role bindings and service accounts that are both identities and resources. Knowing it prevents common mistakes such as broad roles and exported keys.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>The resource hierarchy and inheritance\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Roles: basic, predefined and custom\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Service accounts and impersonation\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Avoiding keys with workload identity\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"hierarchy\">Hierarchy\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Policies are attached to \u003cstrong>organization\u003c/strong>, \u003cstrong>folder\u003c/strong>, \u003cstrong>project\u003c/strong> or individual resources, and grants are \u003cstrong>inherited downward\u003c/strong>. A role given at the folder applies to every project beneath it, so grant at the lowest level that works.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"roles\">Roles\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Basic roles\u003c/strong> such as Owner, Editor and Viewer are very broad and should be avoided in production. \u003cstrong>Predefined roles\u003c/strong> are scoped to a service, such as \u003ccode>roles/storage.objectViewer\u003c/code>. \u003cstrong>Custom roles\u003c/strong> collect exact permissions when no predefined role fits.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"gcloud projects add-iam-policy-binding shop-prod \\\n  --member=&quot;serviceAccount:api@shop-prod.iam.gserviceaccount.com&quot; \\\n  --role=&quot;roles/storage.objectViewer&quot;\ngcloud projects get-iam-policy shop-prod --format=json | jq '.bindings[].role' | sort | uniq -c\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">gcloud projects add-iam-policy-binding shop-prod \\\n  --member=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;serviceAccount:api@shop-prod.iam.gserviceaccount.com&quot;\u003c/span> \\\n  --role=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;roles/storage.objectViewer&quot;\u003c/span>\ngcloud projects get-iam-policy shop-prod --format=json | jq \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;.bindings[].role&#x27;\u003c/span> | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sort\u003c/span> | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">uniq\u003c/span> -c\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"service-accounts\">Service accounts\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A workload runs \u003cstrong>as\u003c/strong> a service account. Create one per workload, give it minimal roles and attach it to the resource. To act as another account, use \u003cstrong>impersonation\u003c/strong>, which requires an explicit role.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"gcloud iam service-accounts create api --display-name &quot;API workload&quot;\ngcloud auth print-access-token --impersonate-service-account=api@shop-prod.iam.gserviceaccount.com\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">gcloud iam service-accounts create api --display-name \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;API workload&quot;\u003c/span>\ngcloud auth print-access-token --impersonate-service-account=api@shop-prod.iam.gserviceaccount.com\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"no-keys\">No keys\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Downloaded JSON keys are long-lived secrets. Prefer service accounts attached to Compute, Cloud Run or GKE through \u003cstrong>Workload Identity\u003c/strong>, and \u003cstrong>Workload Identity Federation\u003c/strong> for workloads outside Google Cloud. Enforce an organization policy that disables key creation.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-the-parts-fit-together\">How the parts fit together\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"n1&gt;n2;n2&gt;n3;n3&gt;n4\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">How GCP IAM and service accounts fits together\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n1\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Hierarchy\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n2\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Roles\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n3\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Service accounts\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n4\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">No keys\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Hierarchy to Roles; 2, Roles to Service accounts; 3, Service accounts to No keys.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n1\">\u003cdt>Hierarchy \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Policies are attached to organization, folder, project or individual resources, and grants are inherited downward.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Granting roles at the folder when a project is enough.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n2\">\u003cdt>Roles \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Basic roles such as Owner, Editor and Viewer are very broad and should be avoided in production.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Using Owner or Editor in production.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n3\">\u003cdt>Service accounts \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> A workload runs as a service account. Create one per workload, give it minimal roles and attach it to the resource.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Downloaded service account keys.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n4\">\u003cdt>No keys \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Downloaded JSON keys are long-lived secrets.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> One service account shared by many workloads.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-key-in-the-repository\">Incident: The key in the repository\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A service account key was committed and abused.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">commit a1b2c3: add sa-key.json\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">audit: storage.objects.list from 203.0.113.50\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">principal: api@shop-prod.iam.gserviceaccount.com\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The key never expired and was valid from anywhere. After revoking it the team disabled key creation by organization policy, moved workloads to workload identity and added a scanner that blocks key files in commits.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Using Owner or Editor in production\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Downloaded service account keys\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>One service account shared by many workloads\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Granting roles at the folder when a project is enough\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"hierarchy","text":"Hierarchy"},{"id":"roles","text":"Roles"},{"id":"service-accounts","text":"Service accounts"},{"id":"no-keys","text":"No keys"},{"id":"how-the-parts-fit-together","text":"How the parts fit together"},{"id":"incident-the-key-in-the-repository","text":"Incident: The key in the repository"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Google Cloud IAM overview","u":"https://cloud.google.com/iam/docs/overview"},{"t":"Service account best practices","u":"https://cloud.google.com/iam/docs/best-practices-service-accounts"}],"text":"Google Cloud IAM has the same goals as other clouds but its own model: a resource hierarchy, role bindings and service accounts that are both identities and resources. Knowing it prevents common mistakes such as broad roles and exported keys. What you will learn The resource hierarchy and inheritance Roles: basic, predefined and custom Service accounts and impersonation Avoiding keys with workload identity Hierarchy Policies are attached to organization , folder , project or individual resources, and grants are inherited downward . A role given at the folder applies to every project beneath it, so grant at the lowest level that works. Roles Basic roles such as Owner, Editor and Viewer are very broad and should be avoided in production. Predefined roles are scoped to a service, such as roles/storage.objectViewer . Custom roles collect exact permissions when no predefined role fits. gcloud projects add-iam-policy-binding shop-prod \\ --member= \"serviceAccount:api@shop-prod.iam.gserviceaccount.com\" \\ --role= \"roles/storage.objectViewer\" gcloud projects get-iam-policy shop-prod --format=json | jq &#x27;.bindings[].role&#x27; | sort | uniq -c Service accounts A workload runs as a service account. Create one per workload, give it minimal roles and attach it to the resource. To act as another account, use impersonation , which requires an explicit role. gcloud iam service-accounts create api --display-name \"API workload\" gcloud auth print-access-token --impersonate-service-account=api@shop-prod.iam.gserviceaccount.com No keys Downloaded JSON keys are long-lived secrets. Prefer service accounts attached to Compute, Cloud Run or GKE through Workload Identity , and Workload Identity Federation for workloads outside Google Cloud. Enforce an organization policy that disables key creation. How the parts fit together The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it. How GCP IAM and service accounts fits together Flow in order: 1, Hierarchy to Roles; 2, Roles to Service accounts; 3, Service accounts to No keys. Step Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Hierarchy Step What it does: Policies are attached to organization, folder, project or individual resources, and grants are inherited downward. If it fails or is weak: Granting roles at the folder when a project is enough. Roles Step What it does: Basic roles such as Owner, Editor and Viewer are very broad and should be avoided in production. If it fails or is weak: Using Owner or Editor in production. Service accounts Step What it does: A workload runs as a service account. Create one per workload, give it minimal roles and attach it to the resource. If it fails or is weak: Downloaded service account keys. No keys Step What it does: Downloaded JSON keys are long-lived secrets. If it fails or is weak: One service account shared by many workloads. Incident: The key in the repository Incident A service account key was committed and abused. commit a1b2c3: add sa-key.json audit: storage.objects.list from 203.0.113.50 principal: api@shop-prod.iam.gserviceaccount.com The key never expired and was valid from anywhere. After revoking it the team disabled key creation by organization policy, moved workloads to workload identity and added a scanner that blocks key files in commits. Common mistakes Using Owner or Editor in production Downloaded service account keys One service account shared by many workloads Granting roles at the folder when a project is enough"},"cloud-iam/workload-identity-federation-and-oidc-trust":{"html":"\u003cp>Long-lived access keys in CI systems are a top cause of cloud breaches. OpenID Connect federation removes them: the platform proves a job&#39;s identity and the cloud issues credentials that last minutes.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>How OIDC token exchange works\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Claims that matter: issuer, audience and subject\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Writing a narrow trust policy\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Debugging a failed exchange\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-flow\">The flow\u003c/h2>\n\u003col>\n\u003cli>The job requests an ID token from its platform, with an audience such as \u003ccode>sts.amazonaws.com\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>The token is a signed JWT with claims about the issuer, subject, repository, branch and more.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>The cloud checks the signature with the issuer&#39;s public keys and compares the claims with the trust policy.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>If they match, it returns temporary credentials.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ol>\n\u003ch2 id=\"a-narrow-trust-policy\">A narrow trust policy\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"json\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"{\n  &quot;Version&quot;: &quot;2012-10-17&quot;,\n  &quot;Statement&quot;: [{\n    &quot;Effect&quot;: &quot;Allow&quot;,\n    &quot;Principal&quot;: {&quot;Federated&quot;: &quot;arn:aws:iam::123456789012:oidc-provider/token.actions.example.com&quot;},\n    &quot;Action&quot;: &quot;sts:AssumeRoleWithWebIdentity&quot;,\n    &quot;Condition&quot;: {\n      &quot;StringEquals&quot;: {\n        &quot;token.actions.example.com:aud&quot;: &quot;sts.amazonaws.com&quot;,\n        &quot;token.actions.example.com:sub&quot;: &quot;repo:acme/shop:ref:refs/heads/main&quot;\n      }\n    }\n  }]\n}\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;Version&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;2012-10-17&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;Statement&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">[\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;Effect&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Allow&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;Principal&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;Federated&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;arn:aws:iam::123456789012:oidc-provider/token.actions.example.com&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;Action&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;sts:AssumeRoleWithWebIdentity&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;Condition&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;StringEquals&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;token.actions.example.com:aud&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;sts.amazonaws.com&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;token.actions.example.com:sub&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;repo:acme/shop:ref:refs/heads/main&quot;\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">]\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Always check \u003cstrong>audience\u003c/strong> and \u003cstrong>subject\u003c/strong>. Use exact \u003ccode>StringEquals\u003c/code> where you can. A wildcard on the subject, such as \u003ccode>repo:acme/*\u003c/code>, lets every repository assume the role.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"inspect-and-debug\">Inspect and debug\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"# decode a token payload (never share tokens)\necho &quot;$TOKEN&quot; | cut -d. -f2 | base64 -d 2&gt;/dev/null | jq '{iss,aud,sub}'\naws sts get-caller-identity\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># decode a token payload (never share tokens)\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">echo\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$TOKEN\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span> | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">cut\u003c/span> -d. -f2 | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">base64\u003c/span> -d 2&gt;/dev/null | jq \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;{iss,aud,sub}&#x27;\u003c/span>\naws sts get-caller-identity\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Common failures are a wrong audience, a subject that differs from the policy because of the environment or branch name, an issuer certificate change or a clock difference. Compare the token&#39;s claims with the policy line by line.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"operate\">Operate\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Give each pipeline a separate role with minimal permissions, log every assumed role session and alert on use from unexpected subjects.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-the-parts-fit-together\">How the parts fit together\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"n1&gt;n2;n2&gt;n3;n3&gt;n4\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">How Workload identity federation and OIDC trust fits together\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n1\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">The flow\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n2\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">A narrow trust policy\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n3\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Inspect and debug\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n4\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Operate\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, The flow to A narrow trust policy; 2, A narrow trust policy to Inspect and debug; 3, Inspect and debug to Operate.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-security\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Security\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n1\">\u003cdt>The flow \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> The job requests an ID token from its platform, with an audience such as sts.amazonaws.com.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Wildcard subjects in trust policies.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n2\">\u003cdt>A narrow trust policy \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Always check audience and subject. Use exact StringEquals where you can.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Not checking the audience.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n3\">\u003cdt>Inspect and debug \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Common failures are a wrong audience, a subject that differs from the policy because of the environment or branch name, an issuer certificate change or a clock...\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Check the Common mistakes list below this diagram for what usually goes wrong here.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n4\">\u003cdt>Operate \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Give each pipeline a separate role with minimal permissions, log every assumed role session and alert on use from unexpected subjects.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> One role for all pipelines.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-any-repository-could-deploy\">Incident: Any repository could deploy\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A trust policy used a wildcard subject.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Condition: sub StringLike repo:acme/*\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">AssumeRoleWithWebIdentity by repo:acme/playground\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">role assumed: prod-deploy\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A test repository could assume the production deploy role because the subject condition matched the whole organization. The team narrowed the condition to one repository and branch, split roles by environment and added alerts for new subjects.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Wildcard subjects in trust policies\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Not checking the audience\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>One role for all pipelines\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Keeping a stored key as a fallback\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"the-flow","text":"The flow"},{"id":"a-narrow-trust-policy","text":"A narrow trust policy"},{"id":"inspect-and-debug","text":"Inspect and debug"},{"id":"operate","text":"Operate"},{"id":"how-the-parts-fit-together","text":"How the parts fit together"},{"id":"incident-any-repository-could-deploy","text":"Incident: Any repository could deploy"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"AWS IAM roles for OIDC identity providers","u":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/id_roles_providers_create_oidc.html"},{"t":"Google workload identity federation","u":"https://cloud.google.com/iam/docs/workload-identity-federation"}],"text":"Long-lived access keys in CI systems are a top cause of cloud breaches. OpenID Connect federation removes them: the platform proves a job&#39;s identity and the cloud issues credentials that last minutes. What you will learn How OIDC token exchange works Claims that matter: issuer, audience and subject Writing a narrow trust policy Debugging a failed exchange The flow The job requests an ID token from its platform, with an audience such as sts.amazonaws.com . The token is a signed JWT with claims about the issuer, subject, repository, branch and more. The cloud checks the signature with the issuer&#39;s public keys and compares the claims with the trust policy. If they match, it returns temporary credentials. A narrow trust policy { \"Version\" : \"2012-10-17\" , \"Statement\" : [ { \"Effect\" : \"Allow\" , \"Principal\" : { \"Federated\" : \"arn:aws:iam::123456789012:oidc-provider/token.actions.example.com\" } , \"Action\" : \"sts:AssumeRoleWithWebIdentity\" , \"Condition\" : { \"StringEquals\" : { \"token.actions.example.com:aud\" : \"sts.amazonaws.com\" , \"token.actions.example.com:sub\" : \"repo:acme/shop:ref:refs/heads/main\" } } } ] } Always check audience and subject . Use exact StringEquals where you can. A wildcard on the subject, such as repo:acme/* , lets every repository assume the role. Inspect and debug # decode a token payload (never share tokens) echo \" $TOKEN \" | cut -d. -f2 | base64 -d 2>/dev/null | jq &#x27;{iss,aud,sub}&#x27; aws sts get-caller-identity Common failures are a wrong audience, a subject that differs from the policy because of the environment or branch name, an issuer certificate change or a clock difference. Compare the token&#39;s claims with the policy line by line. Operate Give each pipeline a separate role with minimal permissions, log every assumed role session and alert on use from unexpected subjects. How the parts fit together The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it. How Workload identity federation and OIDC trust fits together Flow in order: 1, The flow to A narrow trust policy; 2, A narrow trust policy to Inspect and debug; 3, Inspect and debug to Operate. Step Security Observability Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. The flow Step What it does: The job requests an ID token from its platform, with an audience such as sts.amazonaws.com. If it fails or is weak: Wildcard subjects in trust policies. A narrow trust policy Security What it does: Always check audience and subject. Use exact StringEquals where you can. If it fails or is weak: Not checking the audience. Inspect and debug Observability What it does: Common failures are a wrong audience, a subject that differs from the policy because of the environment or branch name, an issuer certificate change or a clock... If it fails or is weak: Check the Common mistakes list below this diagram for what usually goes wrong here. Operate Step What it does: Give each pipeline a separate role with minimal permissions, log every assumed role session and alert on use from unexpected subjects. If it fails or is weak: One role for all pipelines. Incident: Any repository could deploy Incident A trust policy used a wildcard subject. Condition: sub StringLike repo:acme/* AssumeRoleWithWebIdentity by repo:acme/playground role assumed: prod-deploy A test repository could assume the production deploy role because the subject condition matched the whole organization. The team narrowed the condition to one repository and branch, split roles by environment and added alerts for new subjects. Common mistakes Wildcard subjects in trust policies Not checking the audience One role for all pipelines Keeping a stored key as a fallback"},"cloud-iam/permission-boundaries-scps-and-guardrails":{"html":"\u003cp>Giving teams freedom to create roles is fast, but it means an administrator in one account could grant anything. Guardrails set limits above that level so freedom stays inside safe borders.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>SCPs, permission boundaries and organization policies compared\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Effective permissions as an intersection\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Writing safe deny guardrails\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Testing before rollout\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"three-kinds-of-limit\">Three kinds of limit\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Service control policy (SCP):\u003c/strong> applies to all principals in the accounts it is attached to and never grants access, only limits it.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Permission boundary:\u003c/strong> attached to a role or user to cap the permissions that identity can ever have, even if its policies say more.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Organization policy (Google Cloud) or Azure Policy:\u003c/strong> enforce settings such as allowed regions or disabled keys.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cp>The effective permission is what is \u003cstrong>allowed by the identity policy and not blocked by\u003c/strong> any boundary or SCP. An explicit deny always wins.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"an-example-guardrail\">An example guardrail\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"json\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"{\n  &quot;Version&quot;: &quot;2012-10-17&quot;,\n  &quot;Statement&quot;: [{\n    &quot;Sid&quot;: &quot;ProtectAuditTrail&quot;,\n    &quot;Effect&quot;: &quot;Deny&quot;,\n    &quot;Action&quot;: [\n      &quot;cloudtrail:StopLogging&quot;,\n      &quot;cloudtrail:DeleteTrail&quot;,\n      &quot;config:StopConfigurationRecorder&quot;\n    ],\n    &quot;Resource&quot;: &quot;*&quot;\n  }, {\n    &quot;Sid&quot;: &quot;OnlyApprovedRegions&quot;,\n    &quot;Effect&quot;: &quot;Deny&quot;,\n    &quot;NotAction&quot;: [&quot;iam:*&quot;, &quot;organizations:*&quot;, &quot;support:*&quot;],\n    &quot;Resource&quot;: &quot;*&quot;,\n    &quot;Condition&quot;: {&quot;StringNotEquals&quot;: {&quot;aws:RequestedRegion&quot;: [&quot;eu-west-1&quot;, &quot;eu-central-1&quot;]}}\n  }]\n}\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;Version&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;2012-10-17&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;Statement&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">[\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;Sid&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;ProtectAuditTrail&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;Effect&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Deny&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;Action&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">[\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;cloudtrail:StopLogging&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;cloudtrail:DeleteTrail&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;config:StopConfigurationRecorder&quot;\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">]\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;Resource&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;*&quot;\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;Sid&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;OnlyApprovedRegions&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;Effect&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Deny&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;NotAction&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">[\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;iam:*&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;organizations:*&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;support:*&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">]\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;Resource&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;*&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;Condition&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;StringNotEquals&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;aws:RequestedRegion&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">[\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;eu-west-1&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;eu-central-1&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">]\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">]\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"test-before-enforcing\">Test before enforcing\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Apply to a test organizational unit first and check what would be blocked using access logs. Document break-glass exceptions with a time limit and approvals. Version guardrails in code and review them like application changes.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"aws organizations list-policies --filter SERVICE_CONTROL_POLICY\naws iam simulate-principal-policy --policy-source-arn &quot;$ROLE_ARN&quot; --action-names cloudtrail:StopLogging\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">aws organizations list-policies --filter SERVICE_CONTROL_POLICY\naws iam simulate-principal-policy --policy-source-arn \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$ROLE_ARN\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span> --action-names cloudtrail:StopLogging\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-the-parts-fit-together\">How the parts fit together\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"n1&gt;n2;n2&gt;n3\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">How Permission boundaries, SCPs and guardrails fits together\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"16.7\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"83.3\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n1\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:16.7%;top:25.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Three kinds of limit\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n2\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:75.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">An example guardrail\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n3\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:83.3%;top:25.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Test before enforcing\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Three kinds of limit to An example guardrail; 2, An example guardrail to Test before enforcing.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n1\">\u003cdt>Three kinds of limit \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Service control policy (SCP): applies to all principals in the accounts it is attached to and never grants access, only limits it.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Rolling out an SCP to all accounts at once.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n2\">\u003cdt>An example guardrail \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> .\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Check the Common mistakes list below this diagram for what usually goes wrong here.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n3\">\u003cdt>Test before enforcing \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Apply to a test organizational unit first and check what would be blocked using access logs.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Many overlapping guardrails nobody understands.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-deny-that-blocked-everything\">Incident: The deny that blocked everything\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A broad SCP denied actions that automation needed.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">AccessDenied: explicit deny in a service control policy\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">action: sts:AssumeRole\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">112 pipelines failed in 8 minutes\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A region restriction was applied to the whole organization at once and forgot that global services use special regions. The team rolled it back, rewrote it with exceptions, tested on a pilot unit and added a change process for guardrails.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Rolling out an SCP to all accounts at once\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Forgetting global services in region rules\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Many overlapping guardrails nobody understands\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Permanent exceptions\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"three-kinds-of-limit","text":"Three kinds of limit"},{"id":"an-example-guardrail","text":"An example guardrail"},{"id":"test-before-enforcing","text":"Test before enforcing"},{"id":"how-the-parts-fit-together","text":"How the parts fit together"},{"id":"incident-the-deny-that-blocked-everything","text":"Incident: The deny that blocked everything"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"AWS Organizations service control policies","u":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/organizations/latest/userguide/orgs_manage_policies_scps.html"},{"t":"AWS IAM permissions boundaries","u":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/access_policies_boundaries.html"}],"text":"Giving teams freedom to create roles is fast, but it means an administrator in one account could grant anything. Guardrails set limits above that level so freedom stays inside safe borders. What you will learn SCPs, permission boundaries and organization policies compared Effective permissions as an intersection Writing safe deny guardrails Testing before rollout Three kinds of limit Service control policy (SCP): applies to all principals in the accounts it is attached to and never grants access, only limits it. Permission boundary: attached to a role or user to cap the permissions that identity can ever have, even if its policies say more. Organization policy (Google Cloud) or Azure Policy: enforce settings such as allowed regions or disabled keys. The effective permission is what is allowed by the identity policy and not blocked by any boundary or SCP. An explicit deny always wins. An example guardrail { \"Version\" : \"2012-10-17\" , \"Statement\" : [ { \"Sid\" : \"ProtectAuditTrail\" , \"Effect\" : \"Deny\" , \"Action\" : [ \"cloudtrail:StopLogging\" , \"cloudtrail:DeleteTrail\" , \"config:StopConfigurationRecorder\" ] , \"Resource\" : \"*\" } , { \"Sid\" : \"OnlyApprovedRegions\" , \"Effect\" : \"Deny\" , \"NotAction\" : [ \"iam:*\" , \"organizations:*\" , \"support:*\" ] , \"Resource\" : \"*\" , \"Condition\" : { \"StringNotEquals\" : { \"aws:RequestedRegion\" : [ \"eu-west-1\" , \"eu-central-1\" ] } } } ] } Test before enforcing Apply to a test organizational unit first and check what would be blocked using access logs. Document break-glass exceptions with a time limit and approvals. Version guardrails in code and review them like application changes. aws organizations list-policies --filter SERVICE_CONTROL_POLICY aws iam simulate-principal-policy --policy-source-arn \" $ROLE_ARN \" --action-names cloudtrail:StopLogging How the parts fit together The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it. How Permission boundaries, SCPs and guardrails fits together Flow in order: 1, Three kinds of limit to An example guardrail; 2, An example guardrail to Test before enforcing. Step Observability Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Three kinds of limit Step What it does: Service control policy (SCP): applies to all principals in the accounts it is attached to and never grants access, only limits it. If it fails or is weak: Rolling out an SCP to all accounts at once. An example guardrail Step What it does: . If it fails or is weak: Check the Common mistakes list below this diagram for what usually goes wrong here. Test before enforcing Observability What it does: Apply to a test organizational unit first and check what would be blocked using access logs. If it fails or is weak: Many overlapping guardrails nobody understands. Incident: The deny that blocked everything Incident A broad SCP denied actions that automation needed. AccessDenied: explicit deny in a service control policy action: sts:AssumeRole 112 pipelines failed in 8 minutes A region restriction was applied to the whole organization at once and forgot that global services use special regions. The team rolled it back, rewrote it with exceptions, tested on a pilot unit and added a change process for guardrails. Common mistakes Rolling out an SCP to all accounts at once Forgetting global services in region rules Many overlapping guardrails nobody understands Permanent exceptions"},"cloud-iam/iam-policy-analysis-and-unused-access":{"html":"\u003cp>Writing least-privilege policy by guessing leads to either broken apps or broad wildcards. Cloud providers record what is used, so you can shrink access with evidence instead of fear.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Finding unused and externally shared access\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Last-accessed information and generated policies\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Simulating and staging changes\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A regular review process\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-the-tools-show\">What the tools show\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>External access findings:\u003c/strong> resources such as buckets or roles reachable from outside your organization.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Unused access findings:\u003c/strong> roles, users and permissions with no use in a chosen period, such as 90 days.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Last accessed data:\u003c/strong> when a service or action was last used by a principal.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"aws accessanalyzer list-findings-v2 --analyzer-arn &quot;$ANALYZER&quot; \\\n  --filter '{&quot;findingType&quot;: {&quot;eq&quot;: [&quot;UnusedPermission&quot;]}}' | jq '.findings[].resource'\naws iam generate-service-last-accessed-details --arn &quot;$ROLE_ARN&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">aws accessanalyzer list-findings-v2 --analyzer-arn \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$ANALYZER\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span> \\\n  --filter \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;{&quot;findingType&quot;: {&quot;eq&quot;: [&quot;UnusedPermission&quot;]}}&#x27;\u003c/span> | jq \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;.findings[].resource&#x27;\u003c/span>\naws iam generate-service-last-accessed-details --arn \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$ROLE_ARN\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"generate-a-tighter-policy\">Generate a tighter policy\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Providers can analyze CloudTrail or audit logs for a role over a period and propose a policy limited to the actions seen. Review it for rare but needed actions, such as monthly jobs or failover, before applying.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"roll-out-in-stages\">Roll out in stages\u003c/h2>\n\u003col>\n\u003cli>Create the new, smaller policy beside the old one.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Use the \u003cstrong>simulator\u003c/strong> to check key actions.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Attach the new policy, remove the old one for a test role first and watch for denied calls.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Remove unneeded permissions in small steps with a rollback ready.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ol>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"aws iam simulate-principal-policy --policy-source-arn &quot;$ROLE_ARN&quot; \\\n  --action-names s3:GetObject s3:PutObject --resource-arns &quot;arn:aws:s3:::shop-data/*&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">aws iam simulate-principal-policy --policy-source-arn \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$ROLE_ARN\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span> \\\n  --action-names s3:GetObject s3:PutObject --resource-arns \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;arn:aws:s3:::shop-data/*&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"review-regularly\">Review regularly\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Run unused access reports monthly, assign each finding to an owner and track closure. Review external access findings immediately and document any intentional public resource.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-the-parts-fit-together\">How the parts fit together\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"n1&gt;n2;n2&gt;n3;n3&gt;n4\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">How IAM policy analysis and unused access fits together\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n1\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">What the tools show\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n2\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Generate a tighter policy\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n3\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Roll out in stages\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n4\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Review regularly\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, What the tools show to Generate a tighter policy; 2, Generate a tighter policy to Roll out in stages; 3, Roll out in stages to Review regularly.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-security\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Security\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n1\">\u003cdt>What the tools show \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> External access findings: resources such as buckets or roles reachable from outside your organization.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Ignoring external access findings.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n2\">\u003cdt>Generate a tighter policy \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Providers can analyze CloudTrail or audit logs for a role over a period and propose a policy limited to the actions seen.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Removing permissions without checking rare jobs.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n3\">\u003cdt>Roll out in stages \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Create the new, smaller policy beside the old one.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Check the Common mistakes list below this diagram for what usually goes wrong here.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n4\">\u003cdt>Review regularly \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Run unused access reports monthly, assign each finding to an owner and track closure.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Applying generated policies without review.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-eleven-permissions-in-use\">Incident: Eleven permissions in use\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A role had hundreds of permissions and used almost none.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">role: ci-deploy\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">services allowed: 38\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">services used in 90 days: 4 (11 actions)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The role came from a template that granted broad access to get started. Nobody went back. Generating a policy from the audit logs and testing it in staging cut the role to what the pipeline needed, and the team scheduled a quarterly review.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Never reviewing roles after launch\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Removing permissions without checking rare jobs\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Ignoring external access findings\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Applying generated policies without review\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"what-the-tools-show","text":"What the tools show"},{"id":"generate-a-tighter-policy","text":"Generate a tighter policy"},{"id":"roll-out-in-stages","text":"Roll out in stages"},{"id":"review-regularly","text":"Review regularly"},{"id":"how-the-parts-fit-together","text":"How the parts fit together"},{"id":"incident-eleven-permissions-in-use","text":"Incident: Eleven permissions in use"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"AWS IAM Access Analyzer","u":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/what-is-access-analyzer.html"},{"t":"Google Cloud policy analyzer","u":"https://cloud.google.com/policy-intelligence/docs/policy-analyzer-overview"}],"text":"Writing least-privilege policy by guessing leads to either broken apps or broad wildcards. Cloud providers record what is used, so you can shrink access with evidence instead of fear. What you will learn Finding unused and externally shared access Last-accessed information and generated policies Simulating and staging changes A regular review process What the tools show External access findings: resources such as buckets or roles reachable from outside your organization. Unused access findings: roles, users and permissions with no use in a chosen period, such as 90 days. Last accessed data: when a service or action was last used by a principal. aws accessanalyzer list-findings-v2 --analyzer-arn \" $ANALYZER \" \\ --filter &#x27;{\"findingType\": {\"eq\": [\"UnusedPermission\"]}}&#x27; | jq &#x27;.findings[].resource&#x27; aws iam generate-service-last-accessed-details --arn \" $ROLE_ARN \" Generate a tighter policy Providers can analyze CloudTrail or audit logs for a role over a period and propose a policy limited to the actions seen. Review it for rare but needed actions, such as monthly jobs or failover, before applying. Roll out in stages Create the new, smaller policy beside the old one. Use the simulator to check key actions. Attach the new policy, remove the old one for a test role first and watch for denied calls. Remove unneeded permissions in small steps with a rollback ready. aws iam simulate-principal-policy --policy-source-arn \" $ROLE_ARN \" \\ --action-names s3:GetObject s3:PutObject --resource-arns \"arn:aws:s3:::shop-data/*\" Review regularly Run unused access reports monthly, assign each finding to an owner and track closure. Review external access findings immediately and document any intentional public resource. How the parts fit together The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it. How IAM policy analysis and unused access fits together Flow in order: 1, What the tools show to Generate a tighter policy; 2, Generate a tighter policy to Roll out in stages; 3, Roll out in stages to Review regularly. Step Security Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. What the tools show Step What it does: External access findings: resources such as buckets or roles reachable from outside your organization. If it fails or is weak: Ignoring external access findings. Generate a tighter policy Security What it does: Providers can analyze CloudTrail or audit logs for a role over a period and propose a policy limited to the actions seen. If it fails or is weak: Removing permissions without checking rare jobs. Roll out in stages Step What it does: Create the new, smaller policy beside the old one. If it fails or is weak: Check the Common mistakes list below this diagram for what usually goes wrong here. Review regularly Step What it does: Run unused access reports monthly, assign each finding to an owner and track closure. If it fails or is weak: Applying generated policies without review. Incident: Eleven permissions in use Incident A role had hundreds of permissions and used almost none. role: ci-deploy services allowed: 38 services used in 90 days: 4 (11 actions) The role came from a template that granted broad access to get started. Nobody went back. Generating a policy from the audit logs and testing it in staging cut the role to what the pipeline needed, and the team scheduled a quarterly review. Common mistakes Never reviewing roles after launch Removing permissions without checking rare jobs Ignoring external access findings Applying generated policies without review"}});